▸ Request & response anatomy
----------------------------
- When you type google.com, your browser sends a request across the internet and receives back an HTML page
  to display — that exchange is the web.
🌍 Internet: The global physical network of computers, cables, and routers connected together.
🌐 Web (WWW): A service running on the internet — web pages, links, browsers.
📧 Email: Another service on the internet — completely separate from the Web.
----------------------------
How the Web Works ⚙️
(1) what the web is built from — HTML, CSS, JavaScript — and 
(2) how a web page actually travels from the server to your browse
----------------------------
1- type url in web browser ( check Browser cache first )
2- DNS Lookup (Finding the Server IP) DNS translates names to IP addresses.
2- Browser Opens a Connection ( TCP connection , TLS/SSL encryption setup ) 
        * TCP Handshake
              - Client -> SYN
              - Server -> SYN-ACK
              - Client -> ACK
2- TLS Handshake (HTTPS Security)
              - Verify certificates
              - Agree on encryption algorithms
              - Generate session keys
3- HTTP Request — Browser asks the server: GET
              request examble
              {
                  GET /products?id=42 HTTP/1.1
                  Host: www.example.com
                  User-Agent: Chrome
                  Accept: text/html
                  Cookie: session=abc123
              }
              This request travels across:
              - Your router
              - ISP
              - Internet backbone
              Multiple routers
              Possibly CDNs and proxies
7. Request Reaches the Web Server
              The server might be running software like:
                    - Nginx
                    - Apache HTTP Server
                    - Node.js
              
              The server processes the request.
              
              Possible actions:
                    - Read files
                    - Query databases
                    - Authenticate user
                    - Run backend code
                    - Call APIs
8. Backend Processing
              For dynamic sites, the backend application may:
                    - Receive the request
                    - Query a database
                    - Generate HTML dynamically
                    - Return JSON/API responses
4- HTTP Response — Server replies: 200 OK + HTML payload
5- Browser Renders — the page appears on your screen!
